---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Invitations">
  <h1>Invitations</h1>
  <p>
    Admins create users in two modes: <em>invite</em> (the user finishes
    signup themselves) or <em>create immediately</em> (the admin gets a
    temporary password to hand off). Both flows are admin-only and audit
    logged.
  </p>

  <h2>Invite mode</h2>
  <p>
    <code>InviteUser</code> with <code>create_immediately: false</code>
    creates a <em>pending</em> user (status <code>USER_STATUS_INVITED</code>)
    and returns an <code>invitation_token</code> that must be delivered to
    the user — typically as an email link of the form
    <code>https://app.example.com/auth/accept-invitation?token=&lt;invitation_token&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/InviteUser \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{
    "email": "bob@example.com",
    "name": "Bob",
    "role": "member",
    "createImmediately": false
  }'`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "user":            { "id": "user_...", "status": "USER_STATUS_INVITED", ... },
  "invitationToken": "9f3c...e2a1",
  "setupUrl":        "https://app.example.com/auth/accept-invitation?token=9f3c...e2a1"
}`} lang="json" />
  <p>
    The user opens the link, picks a password, and calls
    <code>AcceptInvitation</code>. Identity hashes the password, marks the
    user <code>USER_STATUS_ACTIVE</code>, retires the invitation token,
    and returns a fresh access + refresh pair.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/AcceptInvitation \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{
    "invitationToken": "9f3c...e2a1",
    "password": "their chosen pass phrase",
    "name": "Bob Robertson"
  }'`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Create-immediately mode</h2>
  <p>
    <code>InviteUser</code> with <code>create_immediately: true</code>
    creates a fully-active user with a randomly-generated temporary
    password, returned to the admin in the response. The admin hands the
    password off out of band; the user replaces it at
    <a href="#first-sign-in">first sign-in with a temporary password</a>.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/InviteUser \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{
    "email": "carol@example.com",
    "name": "Carol",
    "role": "guest",
    "createImmediately": true
  }'`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "user":              { "id": "user_...", "status": "USER_STATUS_ACTIVE", ... },
  "temporaryPassword": "Tn7-pass-..."
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>Reset password (admin)</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ResetUserPassword</code> is the post-bootstrap version of the
    create-immediately path. With <code>generate_temp_password: true</code>
    the admin gets a temporary password; with <code>false</code> the admin
    gets a one-time reset token they can deliver themselves.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-sign-in">First sign-in with a temporary password</h2>
  <p>
    A password an admin issued — <code>InviteUser</code> with
    <code>create_immediately</code>, <code>CreateUser</code> in username mode,
    <code>ResetUserPassword</code> with <code>generate_temp_password</code> —
    opens no session. The account carries
    <code>User.password_change_required</code>, and <code>PasswordLogin</code>
    with the right password answers <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>. The
    message starts with <code>password_change_required</code> and a
    <code>PasswordChangeRequiredDetails</code> error detail carries a
    10-minute <code>completion_token</code>. A wrong password gets the
    ordinary sign-in refusal, so the step tells nothing to someone without
    the issued password.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ask the user for a new password and call
    <code>CompleteRequiredPasswordChange</code> with the token. It needs no
    session. When the detail's <code>second_factor_required</code> is set
    (the account has two-step verification, or the login policy requires a
    second factor), also ask for their code and send it as
    <code>second_factor_code</code>: a two-step or recovery code. It is
    checked before anything changes, so the issued password alone can
    neither set a password nor end a session. The new password must meet the
    account's password policy and differ from the issued one. Then every
    existing session of the account ends and the response carries a new
    one. Issuing another temporary password spends any token minted for the
    previous one.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/CompleteRequiredPasswordChange \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{ "completionToken": "eyJ...", "newPassword": "a-password-of-their-own", "secondFactorCode": "123456" }'`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    The flag clears whenever the user sets a password themselves: this step,
    <code>ChangePassword</code>, a reset link, or accepting an invitation. A
    reset link the admin sends (<code>generate_temp_password: false</code>)
    lets the user choose from the start, so it sets no flag. An account
    still holding an issued password cannot be merged into another.
  </p>

  <h2>Audit trail</h2>
  <p>
    Every admin action above writes an <code>AuditEvent</code> with the
    actor's user ID, the target user ID, and the IP / user-agent of the
    request. See <a href="/identity/docs/operations/audit-logging">Audit Logging</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
